<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Camel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/656">656 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">Camel</a> was fought in 656 CE near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali and forces led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>, Talha and al-Zubayr, who demanded vengeance for the murdered caliph Uthman. It takes its name from the camel on which Aisha rode during the fighting. Ali won, but it was the first battle between Muslims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>, the civil war that undermined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel.jpg" alt="Battle of the Camel" width="285" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_and_Aisha_at_the_Battle_of_the_Camel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Many senior companions of Muhammad, Ali among them, criticised the third caliph Uthman for nepotism and for deviating from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and the Sunna. Discontented groups from the provinces came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, and Ali mediated between them and Uthman. Uthman was assassinated by Egyptian rebels in June 656, and Ali had played no part in the attack. The Ansar and the Basrans and Kufans favoured Ali as the next caliph, and he accepted at last, though he preferred to be a minister. Talha and Zubayr pledged to him but then left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> on the pretext of performing the lesser pilgrimage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>, who had opposed Uthman, also blamed Ali for the assassination.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The opposition demanded that Ali punish Uthman's killers, and also called for his removal and a Qurayshite council to choose a successor. In October 656 some six to nine hundred rebels led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>, Talha and Zubayr marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, where they killed many people and took control of the city and its treasury. Ali gathered an army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and marched to Basra, and negotiations in a tent between the armies failed after three days. Ali ordered that the wounded and captives were not to be killed and that fugitives were not to be pursued.</p><p>The fighting took place on a December day in 656, from noon to sunset, and was mostly hand-to-hand combat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a> rode a red <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> in an armoured palanquin, and the fiercest fighting was around it. Talha was killed, reportedly by Marwan ibn al-Hakam. Zubayr left the field and was later killed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>. Ali's troops killed Aisha's camel and captured her. The lowest estimate of the dead is 2,500 on Aisha's side and 400 to 500 on Ali's.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a> was treated with respect, reprimanded for causing loss of life, and sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> with her half-brother Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr. Her defeat ended her political ambitions. Ali announced a general pardon, freed prisoners, forbade the enslavement of women and children, and returned seized property. Some of his soldiers objected, and the orders later became a rallying cry for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>. He then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, which became his main base, and the Medinan elite permanently lost authority over the community. Kufa remained the main centre of Shia Islam until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was founded in the mid-eighth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Camel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ali’s caliphate was consumed by war against Aisha, the Prophet’s widow, and her allies Talha and al-Zubayr at the <b>Battle of the Camel</b> in 656 CE, and against Muawiya at the inconclusive Battle of Siffin in 657 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
